1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,198
Aarav finds an old meter.

2
00:00:02,198 --> 00:00:05,693
Its needle swings all the
way for just one milliampere.

3
00:00:06,043 --> 00:00:10,332
So how can the same kind of meter
read ten amperes, or two hundred and

4
00:00:10,332 --> 00:00:12,123
thirty volts from the wall?

5
00:00:12,623 --> 00:00:16,907
Inside every analogue meter
sits a moving coil galvanometer.

6
00:00:17,257 --> 00:00:21,379
A small coil of wire sits between
the poles of a strong magnet.

7
00:00:21,729 --> 00:00:26,598
Send current through the coil, and the
magnet pushes its two sides in opposite

8
00:00:26,598 --> 00:00:27,498
directions.

9
00:00:27,455 --> 00:00:28,630
The coil turns.

10
00:00:28,980 --> 00:00:30,615
A tiny spring pulls back.

11
00:00:30,615 --> 00:00:32,996
The needle stops where the two balance.

12
00:00:33,346 --> 00:00:36,697
Double the current, and the
needle turns twice as far.

13
00:00:36,697 --> 00:00:39,424
The deflection is
proportional to the current.

14
00:00:40,074 --> 00:00:42,598
Two numbers describe every galvanometer.

15
00:00:42,598 --> 00:00:44,321
The coil resistance, G.

16
00:00:44,321 --> 00:00:48,914
And the full scale current, I g, the
current that swings the needle all

17
00:00:48,914 --> 00:00:49,814
the way.

18
00:00:50,327 --> 00:00:53,325
Now let's turn it into an
ammeter on the breadboard.

19
00:00:53,675 --> 00:00:56,144
A big current would burn the thin coil.

20
00:00:56,144 --> 00:00:59,323
So we connect a tiny
resistance in parallel with it.

21
00:00:59,323 --> 00:01:00,890
This is called a shunt.

22
00:01:01,240 --> 00:01:02,545
Watch the electrons.

23
00:01:02,545 --> 00:01:06,961
At the junction, almost all of them
take the easy path through the shunt.

24
00:01:06,961 --> 00:01:09,079
Only a few pass through the coil.

25
00:01:09,429 --> 00:01:10,732
Turn up the current.

26
00:01:10,732 --> 00:01:15,150
The coil still gets only its small
share, and the needle stays on the scale.

27
00:01:15,500 --> 00:01:20,037
An ammeter is connected in series,
and its resistance must be very low,

28
00:01:20,037 --> 00:01:23,097
so it does not change
the current it measures.

29
00:01:23,747 --> 00:01:25,692
So how big should the shunt be?

30
00:01:25,692 --> 00:01:26,866
Let's work it out.

31
00:01:27,216 --> 00:01:31,507
The coil and the shunt are in parallel,
so they have the same voltage across

32
00:01:31,507 --> 00:01:32,407
them.

33
00:01:32,702 --> 00:01:35,913
The voltage across the
coil is I g times G.

34
00:01:36,613 --> 00:01:41,080
The rest of the current, I minus
I g, flows through the shunt S.

35
00:01:41,080 --> 00:01:43,964
So its voltage is I minus I g, times S.

36
00:01:44,664 --> 00:01:50,441
Set the two equal, and S equals I
g times G, divided by I minus I g.

37
00:01:51,141 --> 00:01:54,019
Resistances in ohms, currents in amperes.

38
00:01:54,019 --> 00:01:56,095
Bigger range, smaller shunt.

39
00:01:56,745 --> 00:01:58,022
Now a voltmeter.

40
00:01:58,022 --> 00:01:59,942
Here we need the opposite.

41
00:02:00,292 --> 00:02:03,572
We add a large resistance
in series with the coil.

42
00:02:03,572 --> 00:02:05,481
This is called a multiplier.

43
00:02:05,832 --> 00:02:09,834
A voltmeter is connected in
parallel, across the part we test.

44
00:02:09,834 --> 00:02:13,992
Its resistance must be very high,
so it draws almost no current.

45
00:02:14,642 --> 00:02:19,249
At full scale, the current I g
flows through G and R together.

46
00:02:19,249 --> 00:02:21,799
So V equals I g times G plus R.

47
00:02:22,499 --> 00:02:27,072
Rearrange it, and R equals
V divided by I g, minus G.

48
00:02:27,722 --> 00:02:28,984
Worked example.

49
00:02:28,984 --> 00:02:34,618
A galvanometer has G equal to fifty
ohms, and I g equal to one milliampere.

50
00:02:34,968 --> 00:02:37,158
First, an ammeter for one ampere.

51
00:02:37,508 --> 00:02:42,634
S equals one times ten to the minus
three, times fifty, divided by one

52
00:02:42,634 --> 00:02:45,046
minus zero point zero zero one.

53
00:02:45,646 --> 00:02:48,589
That is about zero point zero five ohms.

54
00:02:48,589 --> 00:02:49,753
A tiny shunt.

55
00:02:50,403 --> 00:02:52,735
Next, a voltmeter for ten volts.

56
00:02:53,085 --> 00:02:58,087
R equals ten divided by one times
ten to the minus three, minus fifty.

57
00:02:58,686 --> 00:03:02,295
That is nine thousand, nine
hundred and fifty ohms.

58
00:03:02,295 --> 00:03:03,638
A big multiplier.

59
00:03:04,288 --> 00:03:06,669
But what about AC from the wall socket?

60
00:03:07,019 --> 00:03:11,689
At fifty hertz, the current changes
direction a hundred times every second.

61
00:03:12,039 --> 00:03:14,297
The needle cannot follow that fast.

62
00:03:14,297 --> 00:03:17,894
The average push is zero, so
it just shivers near zero.

63
00:03:18,544 --> 00:03:20,907
So an AC meter adds a rectifier.

64
00:03:20,907 --> 00:03:24,811
Four diodes flip every negative
half to the positive side.

65
00:03:25,161 --> 00:03:26,372
Watch the scope.

66
00:03:26,372 --> 00:03:28,244
The input swings both ways.

67
00:03:28,244 --> 00:03:31,071
After the rectifier, every hump points up.

68
00:03:31,421 --> 00:03:35,869
Now the needle settles at the average
value, zero point six three seven

69
00:03:35,869 --> 00:03:37,040
times the peak.

70
00:03:37,390 --> 00:03:42,124
But the scale is marked to show the
RMS value, zero point seven zero

71
00:03:42,124 --> 00:03:43,705
seven times the peak.

72
00:03:43,705 --> 00:03:47,822
Their ratio, one point one
one, is called the form factor.

73
00:03:48,472 --> 00:03:53,532
So when a meter reads two hundred and
thirty volts, that is the RMS value.

74
00:03:53,532 --> 00:03:57,006
The peak is about three
hundred and twenty five volts.

75
00:03:57,656 --> 00:04:00,699
An ammeter goes in
series with a tiny shunt.

76
00:04:00,699 --> 00:04:03,935
A voltmeter goes across,
with a big multiplier.

77
00:04:04,735 --> 00:04:06,199
Now, the exam trap.

78
00:04:06,549 --> 00:04:10,304
Many students think a shunt goes
in series to make an ammeter.

79
00:04:10,654 --> 00:04:11,641
It does not.

80
00:04:11,641 --> 00:04:14,305
The shunt goes in parallel with the coil.

81
00:04:14,305 --> 00:04:17,600
The multiplier goes in
series, to make a voltmeter.

82
00:04:18,250 --> 00:04:19,842
Where is this asked?

83
00:04:19,842 --> 00:04:23,518
In PUC board papers, as
short answers and problems.

84
00:04:23,518 --> 00:04:28,412
And in JEE Main, as numericals on
the shunt or the series resistance.

85
00:04:29,062 --> 00:04:29,962
Try this.

86
00:04:29,937 --> 00:04:34,432
A galvanometer of resistance forty
ohms gives full scale deflection at

87
00:04:34,432 --> 00:04:35,734
two milliamperes.

88
00:04:35,734 --> 00:04:40,741
What should you connect to make a
voltmeter of range zero to five volts?

89
00:04:46,141 --> 00:04:47,041
B.

90
00:04:46,569 --> 00:04:50,216
Two thousand four hundred
and sixty ohms, in series.

91
00:04:50,566 --> 00:04:54,179
A voltmeter needs a multiplier
in series with the coil.

92
00:04:54,529 --> 00:04:57,342
The total resistance is V over I g.

93
00:04:57,342 --> 00:05:02,787
Five divided by two times ten to the
minus three is two thousand five hundred

94
00:05:02,787 --> 00:05:03,687
ohms.

95
00:05:03,773 --> 00:05:08,138
Take away the coil's forty ohms, and
R is two thousand four hundred and

96
00:05:08,138 --> 00:05:09,038
sixty ohms.

97
00:05:09,682 --> 00:05:11,204
Screenshot this card.

98
00:05:14,404 --> 00:05:15,663
Next, ohmmeters.

99
00:05:15,663 --> 00:05:19,332
How can a meter with a battery
inside measure resistance?
